plicity tell you directly how many independent eigenvectors an eigenvalue has?","options":["No. It counts nonzero entries in the matrix.","No. It counts root repetition in the characteristic polynomial.","Yes. It is exactly the number of independent eigenvectors.","Yes, but only when the eigenvalue is complex."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4eaf993ca4c5617ae601d289","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"la_batch18_algebraic_multiplicity_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What extra equality is needed at an eigenvalue if diagonalizability is to hold?","options":["$am(λ)=0$","$gm(λ) \\gt am(λ)$","$gm(λ)=am(λ)$","$gm(λ)=1$ for every eigenvalue"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d4f5485485cd582cc1b033e3","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"la_batch18_algebraic_multiplicity_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For $(λ-1)^2(λ-2)^3$, what is the size of the matrix if this is the whole characteristic polynomial?","options":["$5\\times5$","$2\\times2$","$3\\times3$","$6\\times6$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b0134cdcec0e365a162ba4c1","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"la_batch18_algebraic_multiplicity_016","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Why is the data $am(λ)=2$ and $gm(λ)=3$ impossible?","options":["It violates $gm(λ)\\leq am(λ)$.","It means the characteristic polynomial has no root $λ$.","It forces $λ=0$.","It violates $am(λ)\\leq gm(λ)$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a840819c133496769fcfc065","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"la_batch18_algebraic_multiplicity_017","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is it often safer to discuss the total algebraic multiplicity over $ℂ$?","options":["Over $ℂ$, every matrix is diagonalizable.","Over $ℂ$, algebraic multiplicity always equals geometric multiplicity.","Over $ℂ$, eigenvectors no longer matter.","Over $ℂ$, the characteristic polynomial splits into linear factors."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3a3700f0867cc90fab57f926","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"la_batch18_algebraic_multiplicity_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does algebraic multiplicity alone fail to guarantee?","options":["That the eigenvalue is a root of the characteristic polynomial","That the characteristic polynomial has that root repeated","That there are enough independent eigenvectors for diagonalization","That the exponent of the root factor can be read from the polynomial"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4830f7224f8d151a3831baf7","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"la_batch18_algebraic_multiplicity_020","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"A $5\\times5$ matrix has eigenvalues $1$ and $-2$, with $am(1)=2$. If these are the only eigenvalues, what is $am(-2)$?","options":["$2$","$5$","$1$","$3$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"454a1343e7f6ff8437cf76d9","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"}]}}